WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
At the Center for Molecular Innovation,
Dr. Elaine Smith and PhD student Jane

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.060
Doe are drafting a new paper. To speed up
the process, they feed their data

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.400
into an advanced AI system called LabGPT.

4
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.600
The algorithm quickly generates the
methodology and result sections.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.460
Jane makes a few light edits, and they
submit the manuscript to a prominent

6
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.480
journal, listing both researchers as co-
authors.

7
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.000
This slide breaks down the immediate
fallout following publication.

8
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:33.140
First, colleagues begin questioning if AI-
generated text qualifies as original

9
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:38.060
research. Then, the university's legal
team flags potential copyright issues.

10
00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:42.180
Finally, the journal contacts them,
demanding clarification on the exact

11
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.180
extent of the AI's involvement.

12
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.260
Integrating generative models into the
workflow immediately complicates the

13
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.480
definition of a human author. It forces
universities to untangle new

14
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.760
intellectual property disputes without
established legal precedents.

15
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.660
This checklist provides a framework for
resolving these disputes.

16
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.080
As the first point notes, human authorship
should only be acknowledged if the

17
00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:08.360
editorial work involved substantial,
creative input from the researcher.

18
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.420
Even with AI assistance, senior researchers
like Dr. Smith must still enforce

19
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.300
accepted academic criteria for co-
authorship.

20
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.560
Furthermore, researchers cannot claim
moral rights over text entirely

21
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.940
generated by an algorithm. To manage this,
institutions have a responsibility

22
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.300
to require explicit disclosure of AI
usage, often through standardized

23
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.480
contribution checklists. Resolving these
issues does not require banning the

24
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.740
technology. It requires updating our
transparency protocols so academic

25
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.088
integrity survives the integration of
artificial intelligence.
